n\nNow I'm thinking through the paper structure itself. The title should capture the core finding about instruction routing in vision-language-action models across different architectures. I'd organize it with an abstract, introduction, related work, then an experimental framework section covering the token-level analysis methods like RSA, R², CKA, cross-instruction probes, attention analysis, and counterfactual perturbations. Then I'd have a vision encoder geometry section examining source localization, DINOv2 baselines, cross-encoder comparisons, branch-split analysis, per-layer RSA, and CKA verification to establish consistency across methods. I'm realizing this needs to fit the CoRL format constraints, so I should condense the 24 experiments into a tighter narrative structure with five main sections covering the experimental framework, token-level specialization findings, instruction routing strategies, cross-architecture validation, and discussion. Let me start drafting the LaTeX document with a cleaner organization that groups related experiments together. Continuing with instruction routing through attention profiles and layer-wise analysis, then validating across different architectures with gradient sensitivity and head-level comparisons, plus extending to other datasets and model variants. This is shaping up to be a comprehensive paper—I should start writing it now for CoRL 2026 using a standard article format.\n\nI'm thinking through the page budget carefully. With 24 experiments and only 8 pages plus references, I can't fit every table in full, but the requirement is that each experiment needs either its own section or table. I'll need to combine related experiments into shared tables and use compact formatting to fit everything while still giving each experiment proper coverage.\n\nWriting the LaTeX document now...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":36880,